# Chapter Twelve — Return to Tolland's

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.1. This closes Act Two with a controlled physical reconstruction under Ward's authority. It establishes that Ruth was on the gallery and that her first account was false. It does not establish the shove, the wrist hold, the gathered pages, or the release. The working crime engine remains unratified. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 35 — Bell's round

= Present: Henry, Nell, Ward, Officer Ames, Bell; Edwin and Ruth waiting apart.

= Immediate pressure: Ward will show the building only what it can prove. Bell must walk the fourteen minutes during which he was the one person able to open a door.

EXT. TOLLAND'S MOTOR COURT - EVENING

At six ten Friday evening, the motor court is closed to customers and open to the police. The gray screen still covers the display glass. Light escapes around its edges the way it did on Wednesday night.

Ward waits beneath the canopy with a uniformed officer and a clipboard. Edwin stands by the service door in his working suit, without keys. Ruth stands apart from him near the exterior glass, where the lamps make her a shape rather than a face.

WARD
Miss Avery. Calder.

He does not offer his hand. He points to a strip of tape on the pavement.

WARD
You watch from behind that. Nobody speaks to a witness while I am walking him. Nobody touches a door, a button, a carton, or a rail. If you want to know something, you ask me, and I decide.

NELL
Why are we allowed here at all?

WARD
You are the property claimant. He found the body. I would rather you saw what I saw than heard it from him afterward.

HENRY
The notebook is in the car.

WARD
Good.

He calls Bell forward. Bell has his cap tonight. He carries it.

WARD
Wednesday. Start at the lift.

Bell goes through the service door. They follow as far as the tape across the vestibule threshold. The vestibule is a narrow box: the service lift on the left, its wire-glass door standing open, and on the right the plain door marked DISPLAY ACCESS. Small numbered police cards stand on the floor between the two doors. Ward does not explain them.

BELL
The lift came down. Mr. Ellery and his sister. I had the carton on the booth counter, so I went out for it and brought it in here.

WARD
Show me.

Bell crosses the court to his booth, a glass box beside the stage door, and returns with an empty carton the officer hands him.

BELL
He needed both hands. He gave her the key.

WARD
Which hand?

BELL
Right. She put it in her jacket.

WARD
Then.

BELL
I said good night and went into receiving for the closing call.

He leads them back across the court and through a door beside the booth. Receiving is a low room with a counter, a wall telephone, and a steel bell the size of a saucer on a bracket above the door. A second bell, identical, is mounted inside the booth.

WARD
How long is the call?

BELL
Three minutes. Four. Call it four. The night desk reads back every door, so it's four.

WARD
Then.

BELL
I crossed the court to check the windows. All of them, from the far end back. I do the unfinished one last because it's beside my booth.

He walks the round. Ward times it and does not hurry him. Bell passes the far windows, where the mannequins attend to their own business, and returns along the glass. At the unfinished window he stops where he stopped on Wednesday and looks at the gray screen as if it were the moon.

WARD
Fourteen minutes.

BELL
Yes, sir. About that. I don't time it. About that.

WARD
Did you see either of them again before the window?

BELL
No, sir.

WARD
Did you look into the vestibule?

BELL
No, sir. I thought they'd gone up.

WARD
Both?

BELL
I thought they'd gone up, sir. I didn't think further than that. I should have. I didn't.

Ward walks to the stage door.

WARD
The lower door. The key is on you?

BELL
On my belt.

WARD
From receiving, with the telephone in your hand, to this door open. Go.

Bell goes into receiving, lifts the receiver, sets it down, comes out, crosses to the stage door, finds the key on his ring, and turns it. Ward stops him at the threshold.

WARD
Forty seconds.

NELL
Would that have been in time?

WARD
For what?

Nell does not answer him. Ward looks at her a moment longer than he needs to.

WARD
Ask me again upstairs.

## Scene 36 — Edwin's key

= Present: Henry, Nell, Ward, Officer Ames, Edwin; Bell and Ruth apart.

= Immediate pressure: Edwin's key made the motor court reachable. Ward needs to know whether anything else did.

INT. TOLLAND'S, SERVICE LIFT - EVENING

Ward takes the brass key from a paper envelope and gives it to Edwin. Edwin holds it as if it has gained weight since Wednesday.

WARD
Take us to the cosmetics floor and bring us back.

Edwin enters the lift with Ward and the officer. Henry and Nell ride with them because Ward says they may. The lift rises one short level. Edwin turns the key and holds it. The door opens on counters under dust sheets and the smell of powder.

WARD
Without the key?

EDWIN
The lift stops here and goes no lower. The motor court is a service level. The stair from the court is locked on the store side at closing; I locked it myself behind Miss Ellery and Mr. Calder. After that, the key is the only way down.

WARD
And from this floor to the gallery?

Edwin leads them behind the counters to the back wall of the unfinished window. A dressers' hatch is set into it at waist height, painted to match the wall. Two bolts hold it shut from the store side.

EDWIN
The dressers use it during the day. At closing it is bolted from this side. I checked it Wednesday at six. It was bolted when your people came.

WARD
Who can bolt it from the gallery side?

EDWIN
No one. There is nothing to hold on that side.

Ward nods. The officer writes.

WARD
So a person on the gallery has one way off it.

EDWIN
The stair.

WARD
And from the stair?

EDWIN
The vestibule. Then the lift, or the court.

Edwin looks at the key in his hand.

EDWIN
I gave him the lift.

WARD
You gave him the key.

EDWIN
In this building that is the same thing.

WARD
While you have that in your hand. A transfer tag out of alterations. "Discarded patterns" written over "private property." Your initials in the box.

EDWIN
Yes.

WARD
Explain it.

EDWIN
Miss Shaw asked me for a description that would not be inspected at the dock. I wrote one. I did not know what the carton held, and I did not ask, because not asking was what she was asking me for. In twenty-six years she has asked me for four things. That was the fourth.

WARD
The other three?

EDWIN
None of them left the building.

WARD
You will put that in a statement.

EDWIN
I have. Thursday. Your sergeant has it.

Ward takes the key back and returns it to the envelope.

They ride down. In the vestibule, Ward opens the access door himself with a gloved hand. The enclosed stair turns upward behind the scenery. Its treads are gray. The top ones carry a white haze that thins on each tread down and ends on the vestibule floor among the numbered cards.

NELL
What is that?

WARD
Whiting. The dressers size the paper with it. A sack split on the gallery Wednesday afternoon. They meant to sweep it Thursday. The dressers went down this stair at four; their tracks are under the brown paper by the door. These are on top of it.

He says nothing else about it.

## Scene 37 — One pace

= Present: Henry, Nell, Ward, Officer Ames; Bell below at the booth and in receiving.

= Immediate pressure: Ward has finished beneath the gallery. What remains is to show that a man hung there long enough to be saved by a button one step away.

INT. TOLLAND'S, GALLERY ABOVE THE DISPLAY - EVENING

The stair climbs steeply and turns twice. At the top, the gallery is a plank walkway no wider than a man's shoulders, running behind the scenic wall at the level of the cosmetics floor. Lamps hang from a grid overhead. A sack sags against the upright, split down one side. Whiting has spilled across the planks and been trodden into a gray-white map. Numbered cards stand among the marks. Ward walks only on a strip of brown paper laid down the middle.

Ahead, the rail stops. One bay is open. Its steel lip is bare where the rail should be, and beyond it the white paper moon hangs from the grid, split from its center downward. Through the tear, the six mannequins face the empty chair. Its steel base catches the display lights. The carton is gone. A card marks where it stood beside the open bay.

WARD
Stay on the paper. Miss Avery, you can see the lip from there. Calder, come to the card.

Henry comes as far as the card. Nell stays.

WARD
Under the lip. Look.

Below the steel edge, on the painted back of the scenic wall, are two pale horizontal marks close together, scuffed into the paint about eighteen inches down. Lower, a single long scrape angles across the wall to a point nearly four feet beneath the gallery.

WARD
Rubber from a heel on the short ones. Paint from this wall on the heel of his right shoe and the toe of the left. He caught the lip with his hands, and his feet looked for the wall.

NELL
Twice?

WARD
More than twice. The long one is a shoe finding the wall, losing it, and finding it again. And whiting on the front of his jacket. Two marks, about so.

He holds his hands apart, the width of a chest, and says nothing else about it.

HENRY
How long?

WARD
I can't put a number on it. Long enough for that.

Nell looks at the scrape until she stops looking.

Ward turns and takes one step along the paper toward the stair. His hand comes up level with his shoulder and stops an inch from a red button in a black plate on the upright behind him.

WARD
One pace. I am not a tall man.

NELL
What does it do?

WARD
Ames.

The officer speaks down the stairwell. Bell answers from the booth. Ward presses the button with a gloved knuckle.

A bell rings below, flat and mechanical, loud enough to reach the gallery through the scenery. A beat behind it, a second bell, the same note, farther off.

WARD
Bell. Where were you?

BELL (BELOW)
The booth, sir. It rang here and in receiving.

WARD
Go into receiving and pick up the telephone. Ames.

They wait. Ward presses again. Both bells ring.

BELL (BELOW)
I heard it, sir. Over the telephone. I'd have heard it in the street.

WARD
Forty seconds to the stage door.

BELL (BELOW)
Less, sir. Less now. I know which key it is now.

Ward lets go of the button. He looks at Henry. Henry's right hand is at his side, where it was not on Wednesday when the elevator door began to close. Ward says nothing about that.

HENRY
Was it lit?

WARD
It doesn't light. It rings. It is red, and it is at the height of a face.

NELL
You said to ask again upstairs.

WARD
Yes.

NELL
Would forty seconds have been in time?

WARD
If somebody pressed it in the first ten, yes. A man with two hands on a lip and a shoe on the wall is not falling. He is waiting.

Ward takes the clipboard from the officer and reads from it without offering it to anyone.

WARD
Carton, on the gallery beside the open bay, upright. Contents: one blue folder marked PRIVATE WEATHER. Six reels in numbered boxes, one to six. Nothing else in the carton. On the gallery, no paper. On the stage: the body, pieces of the moon, and one torn corner of a typed page in his right hand. Under the body, nothing.

NELL
The folder is my mother's.

WARD
You will identify it at headquarters through the bag. Not here.

HENRY
Only the corner.

WARD
Only the corner. A corner comes off a page. The page is not on this gallery, not in the carton, not on the stage, and not on him.

HENRY
Bell saw inside the carton.

WARD
Wednesday, a few minutes past eight, when Ellery left it with him. Bell.

BELL (BELOW)
Sir.

WARD
Tell them what you told me.

Bell's voice comes up the stair, careful, a man repeating a thing correctly.

BELL (BELOW)
I ask them to open anything they leave with me. He opened it. A blue folder. Tapes in little boxes. And a bundle of typed pages tied up in white tape, like a lawyer's, with the knot on top. On top of that a thinner lot, loose, under a clip. On top of that was one sheet by itself. Typing at the bottom and pencil under the typing. He said, "That's the only part I wrote."

Ward lowers the clipboard.

WARD
There is no white tape on this gallery. No bundle. No clip. No single sheet. No envelopes, and there never were; Bell saw none. The carton did not fall. Nothing fell but him and the corner. Somebody carried the rest down that stair.

NELL
Paul could have—

WARD
Paul could have put them in his coat in the vestibule. He did not. We have his coat.

Henry looks at the whiting between the stair and the open bay. The cards make a sentence he can nearly read: one set of marks from the stair to the bay, and beside it a narrower set, and the narrow set turned back.

HENRY
Whose?

WARD
Ellery's, in and not out. The other, in and out, and narrow, and it turns back to the stair. It never turns toward the upright. At the post, two marks that are not shoes. Not Bell's. Not Price's. Not yours. You never opened the door.

He hands the clipboard back to the officer.

WARD
Anything you have not given me, Calder?

HENRY
Two things. When she came out of the lift on Wednesday there was a pale smear along the outside of one shoe. I took it for dust from the carton.

WARD
It was not dust from the carton.

HENRY
No. And she said Paul was pleased with himself when he came to her office Wednesday. For having decided.

WARD
That is her opinion.

HENRY
It is what she said.

WARD
Then I'll take it.

## Scene 38 — Where she turned back

= Present: Henry, Nell, Ward, Officer Ames, Ruth; Bell at the booth; Edwin apart.

= Immediate pressure: Ruth has come to show Ward where she turned back. The stair has already shown him.

INT. TOLLAND'S, DISPLAY VESTIBULE - EVENING

Ward brings Ruth into the vestibule alone. Henry and Nell stand behind the tape at the service door, where he has put them, near enough to hear.

WARD
From the lift.

RUTH
We came out. Mr. Bell brought the carton. Paul gave me the key. Mr. Bell said good night and went into receiving. Paul set the carton down here—

She points to the floor beside the access door, among the cards.

RUTH
—and opened it. Typed pages and envelopes. He wanted me to say something to Nell for him. I refused. He said he needed time. I went back into the lift.

WARD
You did not open this door.

RUTH
No.

WARD
Look through the glass.

Ruth looks through the wired pane. The stair climbs into the scenery, whitened tread by tread down to the last one, and the last one whitened onto the vestibule floor, and the floor, between the cards, whitened as far as the lift.

Ruth does not move.

WARD
Two people went up that stair Wednesday night. One came down. That one went into the lift. Bell never used the lift. Price did not, until he came down with Calder. The waiter went out by the Hill Street door at ten to nine; Bell logged him. The cook never left the kitchen. Everyone else at that table was upstairs in Price's sight from the minute you two went down until he came down with Calder. The passenger cars were locked off at closing. Nobody else was in this building with a key.

Ruth says nothing.

WARD
On the gallery there is a red button one pace from the open rail. It rings in the booth and in receiving. I rang it tonight. Bell can open the lower door in forty seconds. Your brother hung from that lip long enough to mark the wall twice with his shoes.

Ruth's hand goes to her coat, where a jacket pocket was on Wednesday, and comes away empty.

WARD
Ellery left a bundle tied with white tape, and on top of it a sheet with a typed question and a pencil answer. Bell saw both. Neither is in this building. A corner of one page was in his hand.

He waits.

WARD
Show me where you turned back.

Ruth looks at the access door. Then she looks past Ward, through the service door, at Henry.

RUTH
Not here.

WARD
No.

RUTH
I want him to hear it from me.

WARD
You will say it to me first. Then you may say it to him in my hearing, because I want to know whether it changes.

He takes her into receiving. The door closes. The bell above it does not move.

Nell and Henry wait in the court. Edwin has gone to the far end of the windows and stands with his back to the unfinished one. Bell sits in his booth with his cap on, looking at nobody.

NELL
You knew.

HENRY
I saw the marks Wednesday. I did not know what they meant until he said "waiting."

NELL
That is not what I asked.

HENRY
She told me her account at the elevators Wednesday night, before Ward had finished with her. In whole sentences.

NELL
People do that when it's true.

HENRY
Yes. They do.

Twelve minutes pass. The receiving door opens and Ward stands in it. Ruth comes out past him, stops at the tape in front of Henry, and does not cross it.

RUTH
I opened the door.

Henry waits.

RUTH
He went up with the carton. I stood here. I heard paper tear. I opened the door and went up. When I reached the top he had already gone over. The moon was hanging open. I went to the rail and looked down and could not see him. The paper was in the way. Then I came down and went up in the lift and gave Edwin his key.

NELL
And said not to wait.

RUTH
Yes.

Henry finds that he wants it to be true. It fits the stair. It fits the marks. He notices the wanting, and it makes him slower.

HENRY
You looked down.

RUTH
Yes.

HENRY
For how long?

RUTH
I don't know.

HENRY
The button.

RUTH
I did not see a button. I did not see anything except paper.

HENRY
The pages.

RUTH
He had the carton. I didn't look in it. I looked down.

NELL
Then where is the packet?

RUTH
I don't know.

Nell looks at Ward. Ward's face has decided to give her nothing.

HENRY
Wednesday night you told me your account before Ward could. So that I would not decide what you had kept from me.

RUTH
Yes.

HENRY
What did you keep?

RUTH
The door.

HENRY
That is all?

RUTH
That is what I said inside.

WARD
Miss Ellery will sign a second statement at headquarters at nine tomorrow. She is not under arrest. She may go home. Calder, you and Miss Avery are finished here.

He goes back into receiving.

Ruth remains at the tape. Henry does not step over it toward her. He does not step back.

RUTH
Say it.

HENRY
You said you never opened the door. You said it to him. You said it to me. You said it again on the telephone this afternoon when nobody had asked.

RUTH
I know what I said.

HENRY
Then I know one thing. Only one.

RUTH
That I was there.

HENRY
Yes.

She nods once.

RUTH
You left yourself room.

HENRY
Not tonight.

She turns toward the lift, remembers that the key is in an envelope, and turns toward the street instead. Bell comes out of his booth without being asked. He unlocks the motor-court door and holds it open for her, the door he never opened for her brother.

Ruth goes through it. Bell locks it behind her and looks at the key in his hand as if someone had just explained it to him.

Henry has known Ruth for several years. This is the first account of hers he has heard second.
